    "body": "The Legnica copper smelter in Poland. ( Image courtesy of KGHM. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie October 9, 2026\nThe European Commission has granted strategic status to two copper projects worth a combined more than 9.5 billion zlotys ($3.46 billion) being developed by Polish state-controlled producer KGHM Polska Miedz (WSE: KGH) under the EU’s Critical Raw Materials Act.\nThe designation covers the Retków-Grodziszcze mining project and the conversion of KGHM’s Legnica smelter into a recycling smelter, the company said Friday . Both investments could benefit from faster permitting, improved administrative coordination and help accessing financing.\nBut reliable access to copper scrap remains a condition for the recycling project’s success. Measures to limit scrap exports and ensure supplies for European industry are needed, KGHM said.\nThe decision comes as Brussels seeks to expand domestic production and processing of critical minerals while reducing Europe’s reliance on China and other non-EU suppliers. The act targets domestic capacity sufficient to meet at least 10% of the bloc’s annual needs through extraction, 40% through processing and 25% through recycling by 2030.\nMining and recycling\nKGHM plans to extract more than 100 million tonnes of ore from Retków-Grodziszcze through 2055, containing nearly 1.5 million tonnes of copper and more than 5,000 tonnes of silver. The project includes construction of the Retków shaft and supporting infrastructure.\nAt Legnica, the company aims to establish a recycling operation capable of producing about 135,000 tonnes of electrolytic copper and 250 tonnes of nickel annually.\nThe investments build on KGHM’s wider copper growth plans. The company, the EU’s largest producer of mined copper, joined forces with BHP last month to seek new copper deposits globally.\nOutput timetable\nStrategic status generally limits permitting to 27 months for extraction projects and 15 months for processing and recycling projects, subject to exceptions, under the EU’s permitting rules .\nShorter approval procedures could reduce delays before construction and commissioning, helping the projects reach production sooner than under a prolonged permitting process. They don’t set a deadline for output to begin.\nLegnica’s recycling start-up is scheduled for 2030, while completion of Retków’s shaft sinking and connections to underground workings is planned for 2035, according to KGHM’s strategy timetable . The shaft milestone isn’t a confirmed first-production date, and Friday’s announcement didn’t bring forward either schedule.\nDespite the regulatory benefits, the designation doesn’t guarantee financing for either investment.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Europe\nCOMPANY: KGHM\nRepublish this article",
